{
"opening": "This release introduces several new utilities and unifies existing caching mechanisms to improve consistency and functionality. It also addresses specific edge cases in argument handling and error propagation to ensure robust behavior across various use cases.",
"requirements": [
{
"id": "m01",
"text": "The public API must expose `has_path` as a function that returns a boolean indicating whether the specified path exists within a nested collection. Additionally, `get_in`, `update_in`, and `zip_dicts` must remain available for their respective data manipulation tasks."
},
{
"id": "m04",
"text": "The `update_in` function must correctly handle operations on empty paths without raising errors or producing unexpected side effects."
},
{
"id": "m07",
"text": "Both `memoize` and `cache` decorators must support raising `skip` exceptions to bypass caching logic. They must also provide `.memory` properties to inspect cached values, and `.invalidate` and `.invalidate_all` methods to clear cache entries."
},
{
"id": "m09",
"text": "The `reraise` decorator and context manager must correctly pass the original exception into the provided callback function. The public names `api_call` and `fallback` must be available to support common error handling patterns."
},
{
"id": "m13",
"text": "Decorators created via `decorator` and `decorator_fab` must properly handle functions with keyword-only arguments, ensuring they work seamlessly when optional parameters are involved. Public helpers like `has_1pos_and_kwonly` and `has_single_arg` must accurately reflect function signature characteristics."
},
{
"id": "m14",
"text": "The `rcurry` and `rpartial` functions must correctly manage keyword arguments during partial application, ensuring that kwargs are passed through without loss or misalignment. The `get_spec` utility must accurately describe the signature of curried or partially applied functions."
},
{
"id": "m16",
"text": "The `limit_error_rate` function (exposed as `throttle`) must correctly interpret `timedelta` arguments for rate limiting configurations. It must enforce the specified rate limits based on time durations provided as `timedelta` objects."
}
],
"closing": "Please ensure all public names listed above are accessible from the main package namespace and behave as described. This update aligns the library with Python 3.9 standards and improves overall reliability."
}
